% A challenge to the intermediate conclusion of a curried implication.
%
% cArg derives C from A (obligation) and B (presumption) via the axiom
% abc : A->(B->C).  B->C occurs in cArg's term only as the type of the
% elimination stack's tail, a context: cArg does not expose it, so a
% challenge to B->C has nothing in cArg to attack.  Intended (the author,
% 2026-10-13, tasks.org aida-curried-intermediate-conclusions): the
% compiler does no logical inference, and an argument is attacked only on
% what it exposes - here A and C.  An argument that wants B->C to be
% attackable takes it as a premise.
%
% Not in DEFAULT_SCRIPTS; the refused moves are pinned in
% test_postulates.py::TestCurriedAttack.
lk.
declare A,B,C:bool.
declare abc : (A->(B->C)).
declare axA : (A).

argument cArg : (C).
cut (A->(B->C)) r.
axiom abc.
elim.
next.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom r.
dixi.

argument aArg : (A).
axiom axA.
dixi.

debate pro open d5 : C.
cArg.
support aArg cArg.
cedat tempus.

counterargument test : (B->C).
by default.
dixi.

debate pro open d6 : C.
cArg.
support aArg cArg.
% `rebut test cArg.` is refused: B->C is no node of 'cArg' (an intermediate
% conclusion it does not expose), so the move carries no verb.
test cArg.
cedat tempus.
label d6.
graph d6.
